WEBVTT

NOTE Sentence timings are approximate, not word-aligned.

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.232
You send a request, then the reply disappears.

2
00:00:03.232 --> 00:00:07.087
In this example, the service already committed the
write.

3
00:00:07.087 --> 00:00:11.213
The caller sees a timeout, but can't see that hidden
outcome.

4
00:00:11.853 --> 00:00:15.514
The same timeout could also mean nothing committed.

5
00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:19.031
Those two histories look identical to the caller.

6
00:00:19.031 --> 00:00:24.056
A missing or stale lookup doesn't necessarily resolve
the uncertainty.

7
00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:27.025
Now retry the same request ID.

8
00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:31.155
With no deduplication, this toy produces two effects.

9
00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:36.377
A retained, durable, payload-bound key prevents the
duplicate here.

10
00:00:36.377 --> 00:00:40.196
If the key has expired, the duplicate can return.

11
00:00:40.853 --> 00:00:45.735
This is an atomic, sequential toy with no late original
request.

12
00:00:45.735 --> 00:00:50.921
Real recovery also depends on freshness, expiry, and in-
flight work.

13
00:00:50.921 --> 00:00:55.573
When the evidence can't settle the outcome, preserve
unknown.
